def edit(prefix,new):
 matches=[(i,p)for i,p in enumerate(d.paragraphs)if p.text.startswith(prefix)]
 assert len(matches)==1,(prefix,len(matches))
 i,p=matches[0];old=p.text
 assert not p._p.xpath('.//w:drawing|.//m:oMath')
 prop=deepcopy(p.runs[0]._r.rPr)if p.runs else None
 p.clear();r=p.add_run(new)
 if prop is not None:r._r.insert(0,prop)
 changes.append({'paragraph':i,'before':old,'after':new})
abstract=next(p.text for p in d.paragraphs if p.text.startswith('Understanding achievement-related factors requires'))
edit('Understanding achievement-related factors requires',abstract.replace('We developed GRADE-Agent to discuss these findings through conversation and brief learning-support summaries.','We developed GRADE-Agent to identify relevant achievement-related factors through multi-turn inquiry, interpret student circumstances, and provide personalised learning-support suggestions.'))
hu='Hu et al. (2026) integrated mathematics relative learning efficiency (MRLE), DML, and MathEff-Agent to evaluate performance relative to learning conditions. Their agent also uses multi-turn inquiry to interpret learner information and provide personalised support. That study reported technical and functional verification, while identifying educational utility as a topic for future evaluation. Here, mathematics achievement itself is the analytical outcome. We additionally compare the educational quality of GRADE-Agent’s final replies with an ordinary LLM through independent teacher ratings.'
edit('Related work used an intelligent-agent framework',hu)
agent='We then developed GRADE-Agent (Grade-performance Reasoning, Analysis, Diagnosis and Explanation Agent) to apply these findings in individual learning situations. Through multi-turn inquiry, it gathers student information, identifies achievement-related factors warranting attention, and explains how those factors relate to the described circumstances. It then provides personalised learning-support suggestions and a contextual summary. These outputs distinguish plausible explanations from established individual causes and retain uncertainty when information is incomplete.'
edit('We then developed GRADE-Agent',agent)
edit('RQ2. How can predictive and DML findings','RQ2. How can predictive and DML findings inform the identification and contextual interpretation of achievement-related factors, multi-turn inquiry, and personalised learning-support suggestions through GRADE-Agent?')
edit('The study makes two connected contributions.','The study makes two connected contributions. First, it combines predictive modelling, SHAP, and DML to investigate factors associated with mathematics achievement within a defined educational context. It distinguishes predictive contributions from adjusted associations, rather than introducing a new estimator. Second, it applies these findings through GRADE-Agent’s multi-turn inquiry, contextual factor interpretation, and personalised learning-support suggestions. An independent teacher comparison with an ordinary LLM evaluates the educational quality of the resulting replies. These contributions differ from evaluating relative learning efficiency and from demonstrating technical operability alone.')
edit('The application used these records to identify areas for inquiry,','The application used these records to identify achievement-related factors warranting inquiry in a described learning situation. Multi-turn questions gathered contextual information, while subsequent interpretation connected relevant findings with the student’s reported experiences and support arrangements. The resulting output identified factors worth examining, explained their relevance, and offered personalised learning-support suggestions. These were candidate explanations for further consideration, not newly estimated individual causal effects. Section 5 describes this conversational workflow and its presentation.')
edit('The study combined empirical analysis with the development','The study combined an analysis of mathematics achievement with the development and evaluation of GRADE-Agent. Weighted prediction, SHAP, and DML examined achievement-related factors in Japan. The findings informed multi-turn factor interpretation and personalised support. A matched comparison with an ordinary LLM evaluated final-reply quality through independent teacher ratings.')
edit('GRADE-Agent links each relevant finding to questions','GRADE-Agent uses the empirical findings to identify achievement-related factors that warrant attention in a student’s described situation. For mathematics self-efficacy, it explores confidence, independent work, persistence, and responses to assistance. The learner’s information helps determine which factors are relevant, how they might relate to the difficulty described, and which support options merit consideration. The output connects these candidate factors with case information instead of reproducing a statistical ranking.')
edit('A decline in achievement or an isolated behaviour rarely','GRADE-Agent’s conversational workflow moves from gathering student information to identifying relevant factors, explaining the learning situation, and considering personalised support. A decline in achievement or an isolated behaviour begins the inquiry but rarely provides a complete account of the difficulty.')
edit('Users first describe an observed difficulty.','Users first describe the student’s observed difficulty and learning circumstances. Follow-up questions explore independent task completion, the onset of difficulties, responses to assistance, and existing support. As users add information, the agent relates these observations to the achievement-related factors in its evidence base and refines the contextual interpretation.')
edit('The agent then summarises issues warranting attention','The agent then identifies the factors that warrant attention, explains their relevance to the supplied information, and provides personalised learning-support suggestions. A readable summary brings these elements together, identifying possible next steps and questions that remain unresolved. The summary presents the outcome of the inquiry; it does not replace the preceding information gathering and interpretation.')
edit('The browser interface supports continued conversation','The browser interface brings multi-turn inquiry, contextual factor interpretation, and personalised learning-support suggestions into the same workspace. Users can describe student circumstances, answer follow-up questions, add observations, and review the factors identified as relevant. The final summary explains these factors alongside possible support actions and remaining uncertainties.')
edit('Natural-language interaction allows users to discuss findings','Natural-language interaction allows users to examine factors associated with achievement without directly interpreting specialised statistical outputs. GRADE-Agent connects the research findings with information supplied about the student, explains why particular factors deserve attention, and discusses personalised support options. These functions support educational interpretation while leaving decisions with educators and other relevant stakeholders.')
edit('The study connects analysis of achievement-related factors with evaluation','The study investigates factors associated with mathematics achievement and applies the findings through GRADE-Agent (Figure 1). Hu et al. (2026) instead focused on relative learning efficiency. Both systems use multi-turn inquiry, contextual interpretation, and personalised suggestions. Our teacher comparison adds evidence about final-reply quality beyond technical and functional verification.')
edit('GRADE-Agent uses research findings to guide areas for attention,','GRADE-Agent uses the achievement-related findings to identify factors warranting attention in a student’s situation. Multi-turn questions gather relevant information, contextual explanations connect that information with candidate factors, and personalised suggestions indicate possible support actions. Its intended contribution is to assist educators in understanding the described learning difficulty and considering appropriate support, while preserving the distinction between population associations and individual causes.')
edit('The comparison suggests that presenting research alongside case information','The comparison evaluates whether GRADE-Agent’s contextual interpretations and personalised suggestions produce higher-quality final replies than an ordinary LLM receiving the same case information. GRADE-Agent received higher mean teacher ratings across all four dimensions, most clearly for suggestion appropriateness and actionability. This evidence concerns the quality of the resulting replies, rather than a direct test of adaptive questioning or individual components.')
edit('GRADE-Agent consequently offers a conversational aid','GRADE-Agent consequently connects the identification of achievement-related factors with multi-turn contextual interpretation and personalised learning-support suggestions. Its value for educational use depends on both the quality of these outputs and how educators use them in practice. Future evaluation should examine how teachers conduct these conversations and whether the resulting interpretations inform support decisions in authentic settings.')
edit('This study examined factors associated with mathematics achievement among Japanese students','This study investigated mathematics achievement-related factors among Japanese students in PISA 2022 and developed GRADE-Agent for educational support. Through multi-turn inquiry, the agent gathers student information, identifies relevant factors, explains their connection to the described circumstances, and provides personalised learning-support suggestions. The study evaluates achievement-related factors and final-reply quality, rather than relative learning efficiency.')
edit('GRADE-Agent makes achievement-related research available for discussion','GRADE-Agent connects achievement-related findings with contextual interpretation and personalised support. Teacher-rated reply quality provides a basis for further evaluation of educators’ use of the conversational workflow in authentic settings.')
